From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dmitry Afonchenko <undermove@yandex.ru>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 14:53:50 +0400
Subject: [PATCH 120/297] Meridian: its keyboard shortcuts on Windows and
 Linux, written as they are there
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Meridian's own commands had shortcuts only on macOS. On Windows and Linux
they now have the same ones with Ctrl for ⌘ and Alt for ⌥: Ctrl+S hides the
sidebar, Ctrl+D pins, Ctrl+J asks, Ctrl+Shift+C copies the link, Ctrl+Shift+E
and Ctrl+Shift+M open the Workbench and Mission Control, Ctrl+Alt+arrows walk
spaces and tabs, Ctrl+, opens Settings. Where that key is taken there, another:
Alt+1..9 for the spaces on Windows (Ctrl+1..9 are the tabs), Ctrl+Alt+= and
Ctrl+Alt+- to split and unsplit. Save Page, Bookmark This Tab, Downloads,
Inspect and the profile menu give their shortcuts up, as on macOS.

The UI named every shortcut the macOS way. Its texts go through keyText()
(meridian/keys.ts), which writes "Ctrl+Shift+C" for "⇧⌘C" off macOS; ⌘↵ in
the command bar is Ctrl+Enter there.

Windows draws the window's own title bar with its buttons, so the sidebar's
copies of minimize, maximize and close are gone there.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
